 \subsection{Automatic labeling}\label{automatic-labeling}
 
-We test whether automatic labeling with SAM 3 can reduce manual
-annotation without reducing obstacle-detection performance. We measure
-the accuracy of its masks and the performance of models trained on them
-without human correction.
+SAM~3 receives each full RGB image with three text prompts: ``small
+object on the floor'', ``cable on the floor'' and ``things on the
+floor''. Masks with confidence of at least 0.6 are united into one
+obstacle mask. For the box detector, duplicate masks are merged and each
+remaining mask becomes a box. No ISOD annotations or human corrections
+are used, and failed or empty labels stay in the training set. We chose
+the prompts by comparing specific item names with broad categories on 30
+reviewed training frames; the three broad prompts worked well and are
+simple.
 
-SAM 3 generates training masks from RGB images using the same list of
-object-name prompts for every image, including scenes without obstacles.
-Prompt wording and confidence thresholds are selected by mask IoU on the
-ISOD validation set, then fixed along with the SAM checkpoint. Accepted
-object masks are combined into one obstacle mask. No image-specific
-object lists, reference masks or corrective prompts are supplied when
-generating these labels.
-
-Each of the five model/input combinations is trained using these
-generated masks. SAM masks are used without human correction, including
-empty predictions and labeling failures.
-
-Both training sets label the full image as obstacle or background.
 
 \subsection{Evaluation}\label{evaluation}
 
-We evaluate SAM 3's masks and all methods in Table \ref{tab:models}
-against reference test masks from ISOD and GTE-Floor. With \(TP\),
-\(FP\) and \(FN\) counting correctly detected, falsely detected and
-missed obstacle pixels, \begin{equation}
-\begin{aligned}
-\mathrm{IoU}&=\frac{TP}{TP+FP+FN},\\
-\mathrm{Prec}&=\frac{TP}{TP+FP},\quad
-\mathrm{Rec}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}.
-\end{aligned}
-\end{equation} We sum pixel counts across images for dataset-level
-scores. GTE-Floor results are grouped by object type, size, distance,
-camera setup and background.
-
-\clearpage+We test frozen models on the 300 ISOD test frames and the 320 GTE-Floor
+frames. Pixel IoU measures how well predicted masks match the reference.
+Object recall is the share of annotated objects at least half covered by
+predictions; several small predictions can together cover one object.
+Because one large blob can also cover many objects, we count false
+regions, predicted regions lying mostly outside any object, and report
+component F1 from the SegmentMeIfYouCan benchmark~\cite{chan2021segment},
+which combines found objects and false regions in one score. Boxes are
+filled in before scoring; Appendix~\ref{app:evaluation} gives details and
+standard box AP. Results are means of three seeds; bracketed ranges are
+95\% intervals from resampling the GTE-Floor recordings.